o a that if they're 8 out  of 10 gave a warning in effect yes a  very clear warning and so if we can  train people to be thinking about those  signs of a crisis and to also not only  recognize them but know what to do with  those warning signs know who to report  to know what intervention strategies are  in place in their work  places or their schools or their  churches that's something we're really  thinking about in terms of prevention  you've also researched mass shooters 200  mass shooters who did not successfully  follow through with their plan  thankfully what did you take away from  that and what else can people you know  who are watching this program learn from  again that idea of attempting to do  something but they were not able to and  I think it also probably speaks to those  who step forward and may have said  something yeah you do find a lot of  stories of people who were thinking  about doing this we're in a really dark  place had seen others do this we're  getting radicalized online were  gathering weapons but someone stepped in  and what is kind of inspiring is  oftentimes it actually doesn't take that  much to get someone through that crisis  point certainly long-term intervention  takes a lot but just getting someone  through that moment it can just be  someone stepping in and saying are you  okay let's reach out is there an easy  way though you think about it how do you  know when it's somebody just spouting  out anger venting maybe just having a  angrier side and when it is something  that you need to step in on you don't  know but there's minds that are  different anything that people can draw  on not necessarily but what we're  thinking is crisis intervention  de-escalation suicide prevention those  are skills that are going to be helpful  in many situations if someone's just  angry if someone is feeling suicidal if  someone's just going through a hard time  not only a mass shooting it's it'll be  helpful in general but we also think it  would